<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">PASOK</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Panhellenic Socialist Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">PASOK, the Panhellenic Socialist Movement, is a Greek centre-left party founded in 1974 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>. It governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1981–1989, 1993–2004 and from 2009, alternating in power with New Democracy and expanding the welfare state. Its support collapsed during the debt crisis, when many of its voters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriza">Syriza</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pasok.jpg" alt="PASOK" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panellinio_Sosialistiko_Kinima_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Panhellenic Socialist Movement was founded on 3 September 1974, after the collapse of the Greek military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of 1967–1974. Its founder was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a>, son of the liberal leader and three-time Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">Georgios Papandreou</a>, and its co-founder was the trade unionist Georgios Daskalakis. Its founding mottos were National Independence, Popular Sovereignty, Social Emancipation and Democratic Process. Papandreou rejected the Venizelist heritage of his father and stressed that he was a socialist, not a liberal.</p><p>In the November 1974 elections the party won 13.5% of the vote and 15 of 300 seats, third behind New Democracy and the Centre Union – New Forces. In November 1977 it doubled its vote and won 93 seats, becoming the main opposition party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In October 1981 the party won 48.1% of the vote and 172 seats, forming the first socialist government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> since 1924. Papandreou had campaigned for withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, but changed course after pressure from within the party. The party won again in June 1985 with 46% and 161 seats. Scandals and austerity then eroded its standing, and it lost the elections of June 1989 with 40% against 44.3% for New Democracy. Papandreou was prosecuted in the Koskotas scandal and acquitted, and he led the party to victory again in October 1993 with 47%.</p><p>In January 1996 Papandreou retired after a long hospitalisation and died six months later. Costas Simitis, candidate of the modernising, pro-European wing, succeeded him and won the elections of September 1996 and April 2000. In 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was confirmed for the Eurozone. Simitis resigned as leader on 7 January 2004 and was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">George Papandreou</a>, son of Andreas. New Democracy won in March 2004, and PASOK returned to power in October 2009 with 43.92% of the vote and 160 seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a> introduced a welfare state, including a National Health System that brought modern medical procedures to rural areas, and indexed pensions to the minimum wage. In family law they abolished the dowry, legalised civil marriage and established a New Family Code that made wives and husbands equal partners. The left-wing wartime Resistance was formally recognised, and leftist refugees of the Civil War were allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>PASOK and New Democracy were the two major parties from the restoration of democracy until 2012, and PASOK dominated the country from 1981 to 2004. The Greek debt crisis then cost it much of its support: it fell from 160 seats in 2009 to 13 in January 2015, a decline that became known as Pasokification, a term for the decline of centre-left parties across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “PASOK” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1974 politics was dominated by Karamanlis's liberal-conservative New Democracy and Andreas Papandreou's social-democratic <b>PASOK</b>, which alternated in power until the debt crisis of 2009 broke them; in May 2012 the left-wing SYRIZA overtook PASOK, and in 2015 Alexis Tsipras became the first prime minister from outside the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The fall of the Greek party <b>PASOK</b>, from 43.9% of the vote in 2009 to 4.7% in 2015, gave its name, Pasokification, to the loss of large parts of their electoral base, and in 2015 the share of votes for such parties in Europe was at its lowest…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
